.TH XYMON-TLS 7 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
xymon\-tls \- Sending Xymon messages over TLS

.SH DESCRIPTION
Xymon messages travel in plaintext on port 1984. xymond can also take them
over TLS, on a port of its own (1985 by default), and the xymon client sends
there when a recipient is written "xymons://". This page is how to set that
up and roll it out. Each setting it names is specified in the page given
with it.

TLS adds, in this order:
.IP "encryption" 4
Nobody on the network can read the messages.
.IP "the server's identity" 4
A client sends only to a server whose certificate it trusts and that
names the host or address it connects to.
.IP "each client's identity" 4
A client presents a certificate naming its host, and may then report for
that host from any address, without being listed in
\fB\-\-status\-senders\fR.
.LP
The plaintext port keeps working beside the TLS port, so clients move
over one at a time.

.SH THE SERVER
xymond takes TLS on the addresses given with \fB\-\-tls\-listen\fR in the
xymond command of tasks.cfg(5), written as for \fB\-\-listen\fR (see
xymond(8)):
.IP
\-\-listen=0.0.0.0:1984 \-\-tls\-listen=0.0.0.0:1985
.LP
Its certificate and key are $XYMONHOME/etc/tls/xymond.pem and
$XYMONHOME/etc/tls/xymond.key, unless XYMOND_TLS_CERT and XYMOND_TLS_KEY
in xymonserver.cfg(5) say otherwise. The key must not be readable by
others than its owner and its group.

The certificate must name, in its subjectAltName, what the clients connect
to: the host name in their XYMSRV, or the IP address if that is what XYMSRV
holds. A certificate from a public or company CA that the clients' systems
already trust needs nothing more on the clients.

Before restarting xymond, and after each renewal, check the files as the
user xymond runs as:
.IP
xymond \-\-check\-tls \-\-env=$XYMONHOME/etc/xymonserver.cfg
.LP
xymond does not start when the files do not load. A renewed certificate is
loaded on SIGHUP, without a restart; if it does not load, xymond logs why
and keeps the one it had.

An expired certificate is still served, with a warning in xymond's log,
and every client that verifies it refuses it. Running
\fB\-\-check\-tls\fR daily, from cron or a task in tasks.cfg(5), shows the
days left before that happens.

.SH THE CLIENTS
A client sends over TLS when its recipient is written "xymons://":
.IP
XYMSRV="xymons://xymon.example.com"
.LP
in xymonclient.cfg(5), or xymonserver.cfg(5) for the tools on the server.
The port is XYMONDTLSPORT, 1985 by default, unless the recipient gives one.
Recipients in XYMSERVERS are taken one by one, so a client can send to a
server over TLS and to another in plaintext.

A recipient "xymons://" either gets TLS or gets nothing: when the handshake
fails, the message is not sent, and xymon(1) says why. There is no retry in
plaintext.

The client is built with TLS when OpenSSL is found at build time; a client
built without it refuses a "xymons://" recipient.

.SH THREE LEVELS
Choose by what must be protected; each level adds to the one before.
.IP "1. Encryption only" 4
XYMON_TLS_VERIFY=none on the clients. The messages are encrypted, but a
client sends to whoever answers on the server's address. Useful only while
the server's certificate is not yet trusted by the clients.
.IP "2. The server verified" 4
The default. Clients trust the server's CA \(em the system's, or the file
XYMON_TLS_CA names \(em and check that the certificate names the server.
XYMON_TLS_SNI gives the name to check when XYMSRV holds an address and the
certificate names the host.
.IP "3. Each client verified" 4
On the server, XYMOND_TLS_CA names the CA that issues the clients'
certificates. Each client sets XYMON_TLS_CERT and XYMON_TLS_KEY to a
certificate whose subjectAltName is its host's name exactly as hosts.cfg
has it. That host may then report for itself from any address; the
certificate allows nothing for any other host. With
XYMOND_TLS_REQUIRE_CERT=TRUE, a TLS client without a certificate is
refused.
.LP
The client settings are in xymonserver.cfg(5); the server settings are
there too and in xymond(8).

.SH ROLLING IT OUT
.IP 1. 4
Give xymond a certificate and a \fB\-\-tls\-listen\fR address. Check with
\fB\-\-check\-tls\fR, then restart xymond. Nothing changes for the
clients.
.IP 2. 4
Move clients to "xymons://", a few at a time. A client that cannot reach
the TLS port reports nothing, and its columns go purple: that is how a
missed one shows.
.IP 3. 4
For level 3, set XYMOND_TLS_CA, restart xymond, and give each client its
certificate. Clients without one are still served and checked by address
until XYMOND_TLS_REQUIRE_CERT is set.
.IP 4. 4
To stop plaintext from the network, let the plaintext port answer only
locally, \fB\-\-listen=127.0.0.1:1984\fR, or close port 1984 to the
networks that must use TLS in the firewall. The tools on the server
itself keep using the plaintext port.
.LP
Senders that report for other hosts \(em the network tests of xymonnet(1)
on another server, a collector \(em are allowed by address in
\fB\-\-status\-senders\fR, as before. With XYMOND_TLS_REQUIRE_CERT they
also need a certificate to connect over TLS.

.SH CERTIFICATES
Xymon distributes no certificates: it reads the files it is given.
.IP "The CA certificate" 4
is public. Copy it to every host that needs it with whatever manages the
hosts' configuration.
.IP "A private key" 4
is made on the host that uses it and never leaves it. The host makes a
request, the CA signs it, and only the certificate travels back.
.IP "Renewal" 4
On a client, replace the files: the xymon program reads them each time
it runs. On the
server, replace them, check with \fB\-\-check\-tls\fR, and send SIGHUP to
xymond. Replace a file by renaming a complete new one over it, so that
nothing reads it half-written.
.LP
For example, with OpenSSL, a key and a request for web01.example.com,
made on that host:
.IP
openssl req \-new \-newkey ec \-pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1
\-nodes \-keyout web01.key \-out web01.csr \-subj "/CN=web01.example.com"
\-addext subjectAltName=DNS:web01.example.com
.LP
then signed by the CA, keeping the request's subjectAltName:
.IP
openssl x509 \-req \-in web01.csr \-CA ca.pem \-CAkey ca.key
\-CAcreateserial \-days 365 \-copy_extensions copy \-out web01.pem
.LP
\-copy_extensions needs OpenSSL 3.0 or later; with an older one, give the
subjectAltName with \-extfile instead.

.SH LIMITS
.IP \(bu 4
Revocation is not checked: no CRL, no OCSP. A certificate stays good until
it expires, or until its CA is replaced.
.IP \(bu 4
xymonproxy(8), and msgcache(8) with xymonfetch(8), use connections of
their own, in plaintext only.
.IP \(bu 4
A certificate cannot be given rights beyond its own host: reporting for
other hosts, and the maintenance and administration commands, are allowed
by address, with the \fB\-\-*\-senders\fR options of xymond(8).

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymond(8), xymon(1), xymonserver.cfg(5), xymonclient.cfg(5), tasks.cfg(5),
xymon(7)
